DESCRIPTION: Seven years dormant, NextBSD is back under new leadership to resume porting Apple's open-source contributions into FreeBSD.
SUMMARY: NextBSD, a BSD variant built to port Apple's open-source code into FreeBSD, has been revived after seven years of dormancy by Joe Maloney, the developer behind GhostBSD's Gershwin desktop. He approached the original maintainers directly and took over the project. The revival includes a new GitHub repository and a history page crediting prior contributorsâ€”a one-person bet on a project that went quiet when its previous stewards moved on.
TWEET: NextBSD is back after seven years of dormancy â€” Joe Maloney asked the original maintainers to hand over the project and they said yes, with a new repo and a resumed mission to port Apple source into FreeBSD ğŸ’¾
TAGS: FreeBSD, Open Source, BSD, NextBSD, Apple
BODY:
[NextBSD returns to dollop Apple source on FreeBSD](https://www.theregister.com/os-platforms/2026/07/18/nextbsd-returns-to-dollop-apple-source-on-freebsd/5273788). After seven years during which the original GitHub repository sat frozen at its last commit, the project is active againâ€”under new stewardship, with a new repository separate from the original. The project's founding purpose is unchanged: porting Apple's open-source contributions into the FreeBSD ecosystem. What changed is who is doing that work, and how they came to do it.

The story of how an open-source project lapses and who restarts it is not incidental detail. It is a structural feature of how much of the digital infrastructure underlying modern computing actually sustains itself: not through institutional continuity, but through chains of individual commitment.

## One Ask, One Handoff

Joe Maloney, who operates on GitHub as pkgdemon, is the developer who revived it. He's already known in BSD circles as the person who built the Gershwin desktop environment for GhostBSD. Shortly after The Register covered Gershwin, Maloney contacted the NextBSD maintainers directly and asked if he could take over the project. They agreed.

The new GitHub repository is distinct from the archived original, which remains accessible with its most recent changes frozen seven years back. The revived project also includes a history page that credits the developers who built the originalâ€”a signal that Maloney understands he's continuing a prior effort, not starting fresh.

There was no governance process, no foundation committee, no competitive selection. This is the actual mechanism by which much open-source software survives: an interested developer notices what's dormant, identifies who built it, and asks.

## What the Porting Work Actually Is

NextBSD's specific purpose is to take Apple's open-source code and integrate it into FreeBSDâ€”work that doesn't happen automatically and requires deliberate effort each time Apple releases something relevant. The seven-year gap in the old repository's commit history is the visible record of what that labor looks like when the person doing it stops.

Apple operates one of the most tightly controlled software ecosystems in mainstream computing, but it does release certain code into the open. NextBSD's work is the ongoing effort of keeping that code circulating in the broader BSD ecosystem rather than pooling only where Apple controls it.

## The Structural Problem a Revival Cannot Fix

Maloney's initiative restarts the project, but the conditions that allowed it to go dormant haven't changed. Open-source projects organized around a single contributor's commitment are structurally vulnerable to exactly this kind of pauseâ€”the work stops not because it became technically impossible, but because one person's time and energy moved elsewhere.

The history page crediting prior contributors signals awareness of that fragility. Whether the revived NextBSD builds a contributor base substantial enough to outlast any single person's involvement is the question the new repository's commit history is only beginning to answer.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;WcXSVffH_GkirIcRDkp17Av2Lp54BI7uj0RmYw6XLUwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;05WR5B9409uT2SvNR6-rFoYmmD0W21aIIwIyiX__A5wƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;2jWL5x5KbVevXRDwU7H0NtmgKE-kyKfOzxrtk5CUAXcƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ERZRbdqmPsdXTRnAN8KifoogyzrCx-2D51QhIC5av7Eƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxclaude-code-cli-invocationsƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;esajVNc8U3QeFatvksQ1QoDTYlMw_qfqSF9kTCGborMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;sRXArsl7NcWAubhRV_quxs2JeYXIK1RLsKUahq_liEQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkg206.742ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;SD9NUhD9t1fODjv9mzSRKga_jq71HUze46wyomKqEuUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;T-2TAttiEN5D6-hoXZUG3GPvvQKEGLA97B8uzbHF9rQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;etW4SXCknLP9C4M9YfA_dVXK30OKvvxw5PKa2vNXN00ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Bjqt3EqV-mYKpvHQeJJnVvTmXgg0sYjpsiRwpUKYYzQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxexchange-20